Existing Solution and Our Solution

Existing / Naive Approaches — and Their Gaps

1. Manual foot/vehicle patrolling by forest staff

Relies entirely on Forest Circle → Division → Range → Round → Beat guard presence
Cannot continuously cover large, dense, remote terrain — especially at night
Poaching is typically discovered after it happens, not in real time

2. "Heat = human" thermal-only detection

Simple thermal cameras flag any heat signature as a threat
Can't distinguish a person from wildlife (deer, boar, elephant, monkey also emit heat)
No confidence scoring or "uncertain" category — forces every detection into a binary

3. Claims of thermal cameras "seeing through trees"

Some existing pitches oversell thermal capability
In reality, dense vegetation blocks thermal line-of-sight — this is a hard physical limitation, not solved by "better hardware"

4. Muzzle-flash-based gunfire detection

Depends on catching a very brief visual flash
Easily missed or blocked by vegetation/distance

5. Blanket facial recognition for identifying people in the sanctuary

Attempts to ID every individual detected
Raises privacy concerns and is impractical at scale/low visibility

6. "One drone covers the whole sanctuary" assumption

Unrealistic — ignores battery limits, sensor FOV, and detection-probability dropoff
Leads to false confidence in coverage claims

7. Alert-on-every-detection

No scoring/filtering — floods control room with false positives (an animal, an authorized guard, etc. all trigger the same alert)
What Our Solution Adds
Gap in existing approach	What we add
Single sensor (thermal only)	Sensor fusion: thermal + RGB + acoustic + GPS (+ optional LiDAR) working together
Heat = human assumption	AI classification using shape, posture, movement, multi-frame tracking, plus an explicit "Unknown" class instead of forcing a guess
"Sees through trees" overclaim	Multi-angle observation: drone repositions and combines multiple views + acoustic cross-check to handle occlusion — with the limitation openly acknowledged
Muzzle-flash-dependent gunfire detection	Acoustic gunshot detection that works even when the shooter is visually blocked, with nearest-drone dispatch for visual confirmation
Blanket facial recognition	Authorized-patrol cross-checking — compares detections against scheduled patrol data instead of trying to identify every individual (more practical and privacy-conscious)
"One drone, whole sanctuary"	Distributed network: Range-level drone bases + patrol sectors + forward/mobile battery stations + tethered UAVs for persistent watch over specific high-risk zones
Alert-everything	Threat-scoring engine (GREEN/YELLOW/RED) that weighs time, zone risk, movement, gunshot correlation, and patrol status before alerting — cutting false positives
Replaces human patrols	Explicitly designed to augment forest guards, integrating with the existing Circle → Division → Range → Round → Beat structure rather than working around it

The core shift: existing approaches treat detection as a single-sensor, single-drone, binary-classification problem. Our solution treats it as a layered, fused, probabilistic system — multiple sensors, multiple viewing angles, contextual scoring, and human verification built in from the start.
